<p>Using the information in the table above, we will compute the following statistics:</p>

<p>(a) Mean \( \displaystyle \bar{x}=\frac{\sum{fX}}{\sum{f}}=\frac{{@sum_fxi@}}{{@n@}}={@mean@}\)</p>

<p>(b) Median</p>
<p>The median class is that class containing the \( \displaystyle  \left( \frac{{@total_f@}}{2}\right)\) item \(={@median_cf@}^{\text{th}}\)item.</p>
<p>Therefore, the median class is \({@float(lower_bound)@}-{@float(upper_bound)@}\).</p>
<p>Thus \(B_L={@float(lower_bound)@}, \, \displaystyle  \left( \frac{n}{2}\right)={@median_cf@}, \, f_c={@f_median@}, \, i={@float(upper_bound)@}-{@float(lower_bound)@}={@width@}, \, cf_b={@cf_lower@} \) </p>
<p>Therefore,</p><p> \( \begin{align} \displaystyle md &=B_L+  \left( \frac{\frac{N}{2}-cf_b}{f_c}\right) \cdot i \\
                                                                             &={@float(lower_bound)@}+  \left( \frac{\frac{{@total_f@}}{2}-{@cf_lower@}}{{@f_median@}}\right) \cdot  {@width@} \\
                                                           &={@float(lower_bound)@}+{@md1@} \\
                                                           &={@ta@} \end{align}\)</p>
<p>The median  \(={@ta@}\).</p>


<p>(c) Standard deviation \( \displaystyle  \sigma = \sqrt{\frac{\sum{f(x-\bar{x})^2}}{\sum f}}= \sqrt{\frac{{@mean_squared_diffs@}}{{@n@}}}={@standard_deviation@}\) </p>

<p>(d) Variance \( \displaystyle { \sigma^2}=\frac{{@mean_squared_diffs@}}{{@n@}}={@variance@}\)</p>]]></text>
